00:01:55,439 --> 00:02:01,479
the more classic engineering example,

51
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:01,479
the electrical network.

52
00:02:02,079 --> 00:02:06,880
Let's start with the most basic

53
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:09,200
electrical component, the resistor.

54
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:11,200
You already know Ohm's law which tells

55
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,120
us that the voltage across a resistor

56
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:16,800
equals the current flowing through it

57
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:20,480
multiplied by its resistance. In words,

58
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:22,400
V= I * R.

59
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,480
We can also rearrange this to say that

60
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:27,520
the current I equals the voltage V

61
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:29,360
divided by the resistance R.

62
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:31,440
This means the current is directly

63
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,200
proportional to the applied voltage and

64
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:35,120
inversely proportional to the

65
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,800
resistance.

66
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:38,400
The name resistor comes from its

67
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:40,480
function. It resists the flow of

68
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,160
electric current. In other words, it

69
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:44,959
limits how many electrons can pass

70
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:46,400
through it for a given voltage.

71
00:02:44,959 --> 00:02:50,760
Next, we'll move on to another

72
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,760
fundamental component, the capacitor.

73
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:57,200
Here we have the capacitor, another

74
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:59,360
fundamental circuit component.

75
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,599
When we connect a voltage source such as

76
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:03,599
a battery across the capacitor,

77
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:06,879
electrons are pushed toward one plate

78
00:03:03,599 --> 00:03:09,120
creating a buildup of negative charge.

79
00:03:06,879 --> 00:03:11,840
On the opposite plate, a positive charge

80
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:13,840
accumulates. Importantly, the capacitor

81
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,000
itself doesn't let electrons pass

82
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:18,480
directly through. Instead, the charges

83
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,120
stay separated by an insulating material

84
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:23,280
called the dialectric.

85
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:24,959
As more charge accumulates, an opposing

86
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:27,840
electric field develops across the

87
00:03:24,959 --> 00:03:29,920
plates. Eventually this field balances

88
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:31,840
the applied voltage and the current flow

89
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:33,680
stops.

90
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:35,840
Mathematically the relationship between

91
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:39,519
voltage and current for a capacitor is

92
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:41,680
given by I= C * the derivative of V with

93
00:03:39,519 --> 00:03:43,840
respect to time.

94
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:45,840
In words, the current is proportional to

95
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:47,360
how quickly the voltage changes over

96
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:50,480
time.

97
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:53,200
We can also write it in integral form. V

98
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:56,480
= 1 / C * the integral of I with respect

99
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:58,319
to time plus the initial voltage.

100
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,920
This is different from a resistor where

101
00:03:58,319 --> 00:04:02,239
voltage and current are simply

102
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,400
proportional. For a capacitor, the key

103
00:04:02,239 --> 00:04:08,920
is the rate of voltage change. No change

104
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:08,920
in voltage means no current flows.

105
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:14,480
Now let's look at the inductor. Its

106
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:16,560
voltage current relationship is similar

107
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:20,000
in form to the capacitors but with the

108
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:22,320
roles of voltage and current reversed.

109
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,720
For an inductor, the voltage V is equal

110
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:26,560
to the inductance L multiplied by the

111
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:29,680
rate of change of current. In other

112
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:31,759
words, V= L * the derivative of I with

113
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:33,600
respect to time.

114
00:04:31,759 --> 00:04:37,040
We can also express this in integral

115
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:38,720
form. I = 1 / L * the integral of V with

116
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:40,240
respect to time plus the initial

117
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:42,400
current.

118
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:44,800
So for a capacitor, current depends on

119
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,800
how quickly voltage changes. For an

120
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:49,520
inductor, voltage depends on how quickly

121
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:51,520
current changes. Capacitors store energy

122
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:54,639
in an electric field while inductors

123
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:56,639
store energy in a magnetic field.

124
00:04:54,639 --> 00:04:58,880
These three components resistor,

125
00:04:56,639 --> 00:05:02,479
capacitor and inductor are the building

126
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:04,240
blocks of classic electrical circuits.

127
00:05:02,479 --> 00:05:06,080
Understanding their voltage current

128
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:08,000
relationships is essential before we

129
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:12,680
move on to phasers which will make

130
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,680
analyzing AC circuits much easier.

131
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:18,160
Let's take a moment to revisit complex

132
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:21,039
numbers because they form the foundation

133
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:23,360
for understanding phasers.

134
00:05:21,039 --> 00:05:26,880
A complex number has two parts, a real

135
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:29,360
part x and an imaginary part y.

136
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:31,440
We can represent this on a complex plane

137
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:33,120
with the horizontal axis for the real

138
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:36,000
part and the vertical axis for the

139
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:38,240
imaginary part. This is like a cartisian

140
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,080
coordinate system.

141
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:42,639
But there's another way to represent the

142
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:44,960
same point using polar coordinates.

143
00:05:42,639 --> 00:05:46,720
Instead of xy, we describe it by its

144
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:49,360
distance from the origin called the

145
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:53,199
magnitude r and its angle from the real

146
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:55,440
axis called the phase angle phi.

147
00:05:53,199 --> 00:05:58,000
Mathematically, we can write this as r *

148
00:05:55,440 --> 00:06:00,479
e to the power ii.

149
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,840
Here e the power ii comes from oiler's

150
00:06:00,479 --> 00:06:07,199
formula which says e to the i= cosine 5

151
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:10,000
+ i * ci. Multiplying R by those

152
00:06:07,199 --> 00:06:12,479
components gives us back XY.

153
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,560
When we analyze sinosoidal signals like

154
00:06:12,479 --> 00:06:17,120
in AC circuits, we often know the

155
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:19,039
frequency in advance. In that case, the

156
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:21,360
important quantities are the magnitude

157
00:06:19,039 --> 00:06:23,919
R, which tells us the signal strength,

158
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:27,280
and the phase angle phi, which tells us

159
00:06:23,919 --> 00:06:28,720
where the waveform starts in time.

160
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:30,400
Instead of writing out the full

161
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,840
exponential, we can use the simpler

162
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:35,600
phaser notation, R angle fi.

163
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,000
For example, if we know an AC current

164
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:41,440
has a magnitude of five ampers and a

165
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,479
phase angle of 30°, we can write it as

166
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:46,000
five at angle 30°.

167
00:06:44,479 --> 00:06:48,080
This phaser approach is a very

168
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,680
convenient way to describe sinosoidal

169
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:54,039
signals. And as we'll see, it makes

170
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:54,039
circuit analysis much simpler.

171
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:59,280
Let's take a closer look at the

172
00:06:57,039 --> 00:07:01,759
imaginary unit I and understand it from

173
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:04,560
a geometric perspective using Eer's

174
00:07:01,759 --> 00:07:07,520
formula. A the i theta equals cosine

175
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:10,720
theta plus i * sin theta.

176
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:13,039
If we set theta to 90°, e to the i 90°

177
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:15,919
gives us i. You can think of multiplying

178
00:07:13,039 --> 00:07:18,000
by i as rotating a vector by 90° in the

179
00:07:15,919 --> 00:07:19,759
complex plane.

180
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,599
For example, if we start with the unit

181
00:07:19,759 --> 00:07:24,080
vector at one on the real axis and

182
00:07:21,599 --> 00:07:27,520
multiply by i, it rotates to point

183
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:30,880
straight up along the imaginary axis.

184
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:33,280
If we multiply by i again, another 90°

185
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:35,360
rotation, we end up at negative 1 on the

186
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:38,319
real axis.

187
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:40,160
In other words, i * i equals 1. That's

188
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:42,720
the origin of the idea that i is the

189
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:45,039
square root of negative 1.

190
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:47,680
More generally, multiplying by e to the

191
00:07:45,039 --> 00:07:49,599
i theta rotates a vector by an angle

192
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:52,479
theta. While multiplying by a real

193
00:07:49,599 --> 00:07:54,800
number, r simply scales its length. This

194
00:07:52,479 --> 00:07:57,440
gives a very natural visual way to think

195
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:59,360
about complex multiplication. Scaling

196
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:03,039
changes the magnitude and the

197
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:04,960
exponential term changes the direction.

198
00:08:03,039 --> 00:08:06,960
Addition is also straightforward. If you

199
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:08,960
have two complex numbers u and v, you

200
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:11,280
add their real parts and their imaginary

201
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:13,280
parts separately. Graphically, that's

202
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:15,520
just adding two vectors tip to tail to

203
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:17,120
get u plus v.

204
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:19,039
With these rules for addition and

205
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:21,680
multiplication, we have a complete

206
00:08:19,039 --> 00:08:23,520
algebraic system for complex numbers.

207
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,840
And this system is extremely powerful

208
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:28,400
for representing cinosoidal waves, the

209
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:30,400
building blocks of forier analysis, as

210
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:31,759
well as for solving wave equations in

211
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:34,240
fields like ultrasound and

212
00:08:31,759 --> 00:08:36,320
electromagnetics.

213
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:38,240
This is why the phase representation

214
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:40,159
using complex numbers is such a

215
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:43,159
fundamental tool in engineering and

216
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:43,159
physics.

217
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:48,240
Now let's see how phase or phaser

218
00:08:46,399 --> 00:08:49,680
representation applies directly to a

219
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:51,600
capacitor's voltage current

220
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:54,160
relationship.

221
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:56,160
In basic DC circuits, ohms law tells us

222
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:58,880
that voltage equals current time

223
00:08:56,160 --> 00:09:00,800
resistance. But for a capacitor, we

224
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:03,120
normally describe the relationship in

225
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:08,480
terms of a derivative or an integral.

226
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:10,240
For example, I= C * DV over DT.

227
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:12,800
However, when we work in the phaser

228
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:14,959
domain, something elegant happens. The

229
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:17,360
derivative operation in the time domain

230
00:09:14,959 --> 00:09:20,320
becomes a simple multiplication by J

231
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:22,560
omega in the frequency domain.

232
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:24,640
This means that for sinosoidal signals,

233
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:27,120
the voltage current relationship for a

234
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:28,560
capacitor can look just like Ohm's law,

235
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:30,720
except that instead of a real

236
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:32,959
resistance, we have a complex quantity

237
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:34,959
called impedance.

238
00:09:32,959 --> 00:09:36,800
Here's the key point. In any linear

239
00:09:34,959 --> 00:09:39,279
circuit made up of resistors,

240
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:41,360
capacitors, and inductors, if we drive

241
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:43,360
it with a pure sinosoidal signal, the

242
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:46,000
output will also be a sinosoid of the

243
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:47,920
exact same frequency. The amplitude may

244
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,519
change and the waveform may shift in

245
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:52,519
phase but the frequency remains

246
00:09:49,519 --> 00:09:52,519
unchanged.

247
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:57,440
For example, suppose the voltage across

248
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:59,680
a capacitor is a cosine omega t.

249
00:09:57,440 --> 00:10:02,399
Differentiating this to find the current

250
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:05,519
shifts the waveform by 90° and

251
00:10:02,399 --> 00:10:07,600
multiplies its amplitude by C omega. In

252
00:10:05,519 --> 00:10:11,519
phaser notation, this phase shift is

253
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:13,279
written as at an angle of plus 90°.

254
00:10:11,519 --> 00:10:17,519
Finally, in the phaser domain, the

255
00:10:13,279 --> 00:10:19,920
capacitor's impedance is 1 / j omega c.

256
00:10:17,519 --> 00:10:22,880
That's directly analogous to resistance

257
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:24,959
in Ohm's law, except here it's a complex

258
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:29,079
value reflecting the fact that voltage

259
00:10:24,959 --> 00:10:29,079
and current are out of phase.

260
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:34,880
For an inductor, the process is very

261
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:36,800
similar to what we saw with a capacitor,

262
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:38,560
but here it's the current that we start

263
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,560
with.

264
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:43,040
Suppose the current is sinosoidal. B

265
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:47,120
cosine omega t. The voltage across the

266
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:48,959
inductor is given by V= L * DI DT.

267
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:51,120
Differentiating the current shifts the

268
00:10:48,959 --> 00:10:54,399
waveform by 90° and changes the

269
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:56,560
amplitude by a factor of L omega.

270
00:10:54,399 --> 00:10:59,440
In phaser notation, if the current is B

271
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:02,880
at angle 0°, then the voltage becomes L

272
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:04,800
omega B at angle 90°. That phase shift

273
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:07,600
means the voltage leads the current by

274
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:09,760
90° in an inductor. the exact opposite

275
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:13,560
of a capacitor where the current leads

276
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:13,560
the voltage by 90°.

277
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:20,640
In the phaser domain, this relationship

278
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:23,120
is written simply as V= J omega L * 1.

279
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:25,440
Here, J omega L plays the same role for

280
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:28,399
an inductor that resistance R plays for

281
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:30,000
a resistor. It's the impedance. The only

282
00:11:28,399 --> 00:11:31,600
difference is that it's a complex

283
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,200
quantity reflecting the phase

284
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:34,800
relationship between voltage and

285
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:37,200
current.

286
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:39,440
So once again, phaser notation allows us

287
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:42,240
to generalize Ohm's law to all three

288
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:44,640
basic components, resistor, capacitor,

289
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:47,640
and inductor using the single idea of

290
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:47,640
impedance.

291
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:54,160
Up to now, we've talked about resistors,

292
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,839
capacitors, and inductors separately.

293
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:57,600
Each has its own voltage current

294
00:11:55,839 --> 00:12:00,079
relationship, and each can be expressed

295
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:01,760
in the phaser domain.

296
00:12:00,079 --> 00:12:03,839
Instead of calling all these effects

297
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:06,959
resistance, we use a broader term,

298
00:12:03,839 --> 00:12:08,880
impedance. Impedance describes how any

299
00:12:06,959 --> 00:12:10,959
circuit element opposes the flow of

300
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:14,560
alternating current and it applies to

301
00:12:10,959 --> 00:12:17,360
all three components.

302
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:19,920
For a resistor, impedance is simply R.

303
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:22,720
For a capacitor, impedance is 1 / J

304
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:25,600
omega C. For an inductor, impedance is J

305
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:27,680
omega L.

306
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:31,360
In each case, impedance is defined as

307
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:33,120
voltage divided by current. Z= V / I.

308
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:36,240
This is the same structure as Ohm's law,

309
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:38,480
but with Z taking the place of R.

310
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:40,480
When we combine components in a circuit,

311
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:42,560
the total impedance depends on whether

312
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:44,720
they are connected in series or in

313
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:46,800
parallel. Later, we'll learn how to

314
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:49,040
calculate equivalent impedance for these

315
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:50,399
different configurations. But the

316
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:52,399
important takeaway here is that

317
00:12:50,399 --> 00:12:54,560
impedance unifies the behavior of

318
00:12:52,399 --> 00:12:57,200
resistors, capacitors, and inductors

319
00:12:54,560 --> 00:13:01,560
into one general concept, making AC

320
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,560
circuit analysis much simpler.

321
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:06,959
Let's now step back and look at a

322
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:08,880
general electrical network. I'll use

323
00:13:06,959 --> 00:13:12,639
this simple example to introduce three

324
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:14,480
important terms. Node, branch, and loop.

325
00:13:12,639 --> 00:13:17,200
A node is a point where two or more

326
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:19,200
circuit components meet.

327
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:21,680
These components could be resistors,

328
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:24,320
capacitors, inductors, or sources like

329
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:26,160
batteries and current generators. In our

330
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:28,399
diagram, each place where elements

331
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:30,399
connect is a node.

332
00:13:28,399 --> 00:13:32,639
A branch is a single circuit element

333
00:13:30,399 --> 00:13:34,639
between two nodes. You can think of it

334
00:13:32,639 --> 00:13:37,519
as one arm of the circuit carrying

335
00:13:34,639 --> 00:13:39,680
current from one node to another.

336
00:13:37,519 --> 00:13:41,760
A loop is a closed path that goes

337
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:43,519
through a sequence of branches and nodes

338
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:45,760
without passing through any branch or

339
00:13:43,519 --> 00:13:48,000
node more than once. In other words,

340
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:51,120
it's a single non-re repeating path that

341
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,040
starts and ends at the same point.

342
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:54,959
Using these definitions, you might ask

343
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:57,839
in this diagram, how many nodes are

344
00:13:54,959 --> 00:13:59,279
there and how many loops?

345
00:13:57,839 --> 00:14:01,360
This kind of reasoning will become

346
00:13:59,279 --> 00:14:05,000
important when we apply Kirchoff's laws

347
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:05,000
for circuit analysis.

348
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:11,440
So, how many nodes are in this circuit?

349
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:13,199
Let's apply the definition.

350
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:15,760
A node is any point where two or more

351
00:14:13,199 --> 00:14:17,519
branches connect. Here

352
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:19,680
all the points along this conducting

353
00:14:17,519 --> 00:14:22,000
path are electrically the same. The

354
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:24,240
wires are ideal conductors with zero

355
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:26,480
resistance. So we treat them as one

356
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:29,760
single node. This first node connects

357
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:32,240
resistor R1, resistor R2, resistor R3

358
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:34,079
and the voltage source.

359
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:36,480
The other connection point down here is

360
00:14:34,079 --> 00:14:39,440
the second node. It ties together the

361
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:41,279
lower ends of R2 and R3, the negative

362
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:43,279
terminal of the voltage source and the

363
00:14:41,279 --> 00:14:44,720
current source.

364
00:14:43,279 --> 00:14:46,560
So even though there are multiple

365
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:48,480
connection spots physically drawn

366
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:52,199
electrically we have just two distinct

367
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:52,199
nodes in this circuit.

368
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:58,240
Now let's talk about loops and more

369
00:14:55,440 --> 00:15:00,639
specifically independent loops.

370
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:02,399
A loop is a closed path in the circuit

371
00:15:00,639 --> 00:15:04,399
that passes through a sequence of

372
00:15:02,399 --> 00:15:06,399
branches and returns to the starting

373
00:15:04,399 --> 00:15:08,880
point without crossing any branch more

374
00:15:06,399 --> 00:15:11,360
than once. But not all loops are equally

375
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:13,600
useful in analysis.

376
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:15,680
We focus on independent loops which each

377
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:18,639
contain at least one branch that is not

378
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:20,079
part of any other loop in the set.

379
00:15:18,639 --> 00:15:22,720
In this example, we have three

380
00:15:20,079 --> 00:15:24,800
independent loops. Loop one includes the

381
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:27,839
voltage source, resistor R1, and

382
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:31,040
resistor R2. Loop two includes resistor

383
00:15:27,839 --> 00:15:34,399
R2 and resistor R3. Loop three includes

384
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:36,480
resistor R3 and the current source.

385
00:15:34,399 --> 00:15:38,320
You might notice that other closed paths

386
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:40,399
exist. For example, one that goes all

387
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:42,480
the way around the outside. But these

388
00:15:40,399 --> 00:15:44,720
can be derived from combinations of the

389
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:47,120
independent loops. They don't give us

390
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:48,880
any new information.

391
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:51,040
The reason this concept is important is

392
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:53,120
that each independent loop gives us one

393
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:55,199
equation when we apply Kirchhoff's

394
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:57,120
voltage law.

395
00:15:55,199 --> 00:15:58,800
These equations are the building blocks

396
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:00,959
for solving circuit problems with

397
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:02,880
multiple unknowns such as the current

398
00:16:00,959 --> 00:16:06,600
through each branch or the voltage

399
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:06,600
across each resistor.

400
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:12,079
The first rule we use in circuit

401
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:14,800
analysis is Kirchoff's current law or

402
00:16:12,079 --> 00:16:16,560
KCL. It's very straightforward. For any

403
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:18,399
node in a circuit, the total current

404
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:20,880
flowing into the node must equal the

405
00:16:18,399 --> 00:16:22,399
total current flowing out.

406
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:24,560
Think of the node as a junction.

407
00:16:22,399 --> 00:16:26,720
Electrons flow in from some branches and

408
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:29,040
out through others. They don't pile up

409
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:31,680
or vanish at the node.

410
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:34,160
If two ampers flow in from one branch

411
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:36,800
and three ampers flow in from another,

412
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:39,360
then a total of 5 ampers must flow out

413
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:41,360
through the remaining branch.

414
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:43,759
You can picture this like people moving

415
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:46,399
through a doorway. The number of people

416
00:16:43,759 --> 00:16:48,560
entering must match the number leaving,

417
00:16:46,399 --> 00:16:51,279
assuming no one stays inside the

418
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:53,680
doorway. or like cars passing through an

419
00:16:51,279 --> 00:16:58,000
intersection. Cars can't just appear or

420
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:59,920
disappear. What comes in must go out.

421
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,839
KCL is essentially a statement of

422
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:04,400
conservation of charge. Charge is

423
00:17:01,839 --> 00:17:06,319
neither created nor destroyed at a node.

424
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:08,160
This simple but powerful rule will be

425
00:17:06,319 --> 00:17:11,559
one of the main tools we use to solve

426
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:11,559
circuit equations.

427
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:18,480
The second key rule is Kirchoff's

428
00:17:15,280 --> 00:17:20,400
voltage law or KVL. It states that if

429
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:22,000
you travel around any closed loop in a

430
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:25,199
circuit, the sum of all the voltage

431
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:26,880
rises and drops must equal zero.

432
00:17:25,199 --> 00:17:28,880
You can think of it like taking a hike

433
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:31,280
around a loop trail that starts and ends

434
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:33,280
at the same place.

435
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:34,960
Along the way, you might climb uphill.

436
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:37,200
That's like a voltage rise from a

437
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:39,200
battery. And you might walk downhill.

438
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:41,600
That's like a voltage drop across a

439
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:43,600
resistor. By the time you return to

440
00:17:41,600 --> 00:17:45,840
where you started, your total elevation

441
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:47,679
change is zero.

442
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:49,280
In a circuit, it's the same idea, but

443
00:17:47,679 --> 00:17:51,679
with electrical potential instead of

444
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:53,440
gravitational potential.

445
00:17:51,679 --> 00:17:55,760
As electrons move through a voltage

446
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:57,760
source, they gain energy. As they pass

447
00:17:55,760 --> 00:18:00,160
through resistive elements, they lose

448
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:02,080
energy. Around a complete loop, the

449
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:04,400
total gains and losses cancel out

450
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:06,400
exactly, a direct consequence of energy

451
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:08,559
conservation.

452
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:11,280
In this example, the voltage source V1

453
00:18:08,559 --> 00:18:14,000
is 10 volt. As we move around the loop,

454
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:16,240
we see drops of 2 volts, 3 volts, and 1

455
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:19,760
volt across different resistors and 0

456
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:21,840
volts across the ideal conducting wire.

457
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:24,720
That leaves one resistor with an unknown

458
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:28,240
drop. To satisfy KVL, it must drop 4

459
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:30,640
volts so that the algebraic sum is zero.

460
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:32,320
When we combine KVL with Kirchoff's

461
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:34,640
current law, we can write enough

462
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:37,120
equations to solve for all the unknown

463
00:18:34,640 --> 00:18:40,640
currents and voltages in even a complex

464
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:40,640
electrical network.

465
00:18:42,320 --> 00:18:46,320
If you start with the simplest case, a

466
00:18:44,640 --> 00:18:48,080
single loop, the problem is

467
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:49,840
straightforward. You can solve it

468
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:53,200
directly just like using Ohm's law.

469
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:54,960
Voltage equals impedance times current.

470
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:58,160
As you make the network more complex,

471
00:18:54,960 --> 00:19:00,320
you can add loops, nodes or both.

472
00:18:58,160 --> 00:19:02,799
Each time you add a loop, you introduce

473
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:05,440
a new unknown. But KVL gives you one

474
00:19:02,799 --> 00:19:07,120
more independent equation to match it.

475
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:09,520
Each time you add a node, you also

476
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:12,880
introduce a new unknown. However, KCL

477
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:15,039
provides the additional equation needed.

478
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:17,600
If you add both a node and a loop at the

479
00:19:15,039 --> 00:19:19,919
same time, you introduce two unknowns.

480
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:22,080
For example, splitting a branch into two

481
00:19:19,919 --> 00:19:25,039
path so currents can differ. But you

482
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:27,280
also gain two equations, one from KCL at

483
00:19:25,039 --> 00:19:29,120
the new node and one from KVL around the

484
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:31,280
new loop.

485
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:34,559
The key point is that no matter how you

486
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:36,000
expand the network, KCL and KVL together

487
00:19:34,559 --> 00:19:38,000
give you exactly the number of

488
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,760
independent equations you need to solve

489
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:41,760
for all the unknown currents and

490
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,520
voltages.

491
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:45,120
This balance is what ensures that a

492
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:49,160
well- definfined electrical network

493
00:19:45,120 --> 00:19:49,160
always has a unique solution.

494
00:19:50,799 --> 00:19:55,679
Let's solve this example step by step.

495
00:19:53,919 --> 00:19:58,480
We have two voltage sources and two

496
00:19:55,679 --> 00:20:01,200
loops. Our goal is to find three unknown

497
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:04,000
currents. I1 which flows through the

498
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:06,799
left loop. I2 which flows through the

499
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:09,840
right loop and I3 which flows along the

500
00:20:06,799 --> 00:20:12,080
bottom branch.

501
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:14,240
First, we apply Kirchhoff's current law

502
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:16,559
at node C.

503
00:20:14,240 --> 00:20:18,880
In words, the current I1 flowing into

504
00:20:16,559 --> 00:20:21,280
the node plus the current I2 flowing

505
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:24,320
into the node must equal the current I3

506
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:27,840
flowing out. That's our first equation.

507
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:30,480
I1 + I2 equals I3.

508
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:32,559
Next, we apply Kirchoff's voltage law to

509
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:36,480
the green loop, moving clockwise through

510
00:20:32,559 --> 00:20:38,640
points B, E, F, C, and back to B. As we

511
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:40,799
go around the loop, passing through the

512
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:44,000
14V battery in the opposite direction

513
00:20:40,799 --> 00:20:46,000
gives a minus4 vol.

514
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,679
Passing through the 10V battery in the

515
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:51,679
opposite direction gives - 10 vol.

516
00:20:49,679 --> 00:20:54,880
Passing through the 6 ohm resistor with

517
00:20:51,679 --> 00:20:56,640
current, I1 gives plus 6 * I1. And

518
00:20:54,880 --> 00:21:00,400
passing through the 4 ohm resistor with

519
00:20:56,640 --> 00:21:02,159
current, I2 gives -4 * I2.

520
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:07,280
Adding them all up and setting the total

521
00:21:02,159 --> 00:21:10,720
to zero, we have -14 - 10 + 6 * I1 - 4 *

522
00:21:07,280 --> 00:21:13,120
I2 = 0.

523
00:21:10,720 --> 00:21:15,200
Finally, we apply Kirchoff's voltage law

524
00:21:13,120 --> 00:21:19,440
to the blue loop, moving through points

525
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:23,840
A, B, C, D, and back to A. This gives 10

526
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:27,360
- 6 * I1 - 2 * I3 = 0.

527
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:33,760
Now we have three equations. I1 + I2 =

528
00:21:27,360 --> 00:21:38,159
I3. -14 - 10 + 6 I1 - 4 I2 = 0. 10 - 6

529
00:21:33,760 --> 00:21:43,760
I1 - 2 I3 = 0.

530
00:21:38,159 --> 00:21:46,000
Solving these we find I1 = 2 a I2 = -3

531
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:48,559
ampers. meaning it actually flows in the

532
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:52,520
opposite direction we assumed and I3

533
00:21:48,559 --> 00:21:52,520
equals 1 aere.

534
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:58,400
This slide is a summary that generalizes

535
00:21:56,400 --> 00:22:00,240
Ohm's law for alternating current

536
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:02,080
circuits.

537
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:04,960
In direct current circuits, we know the

538
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:06,960
familiar form voltage equals resistance

539
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:08,880
time current.

540
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:10,960
In alternating current circuits, thanks

541
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:12,799
to phaser notation, we can keep the same

542
00:22:10,960 --> 00:22:14,720
simple structure. Voltage equals

543
00:22:12,799 --> 00:22:16,640
impedance times current. But now

544
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:19,520
impedance can be a complex number

545
00:22:16,640 --> 00:22:21,919
depending on the type of component.

546
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:24,240
For a resistor, impedance is simply R

547
00:22:21,919 --> 00:22:27,360
and voltage and current are in phase.

548
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:30,480
Their peaks occur simultaneously.

549
00:22:27,360 --> 00:22:33,039
For a capacitor, the impedance is 1 / J

550
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:37,360
omega C, which in polar form is 1 /

551
00:22:33,039 --> 00:22:39,120
omega C at an angle of 90°.

552
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:41,840
This means the voltage lags the current

553
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:43,679
by 90°. In other words, the current

554
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:45,280
reaches its peak before the voltage

555
00:22:43,679 --> 00:22:47,600
does.

556
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:50,640
For an inductor, the impedance is J

557
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:53,600
omega L or in polar form omega L at an

558
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:56,400
angle of positive 90°. This means the

559
00:22:53,600 --> 00:22:58,000
voltage leads the current by 90°. The

560
00:22:56,400 --> 00:23:00,240
voltage peaks first and the current

561
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:02,559
builds up afterward.

562
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:04,240
If you think about it intuitively, an

563
00:23:02,559 --> 00:23:05,600
inductor resists sudden changes in

564
00:23:04,240 --> 00:23:07,440
current. So when you first apply

565
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:09,919
voltage, the current starts small and

566
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:12,159
gradually increases.

567
00:23:09,919 --> 00:23:14,000
A capacitor resists changes in voltage.

568
00:23:12,159 --> 00:23:16,880
So when you first apply current, the

569
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:18,799
voltage takes time to build.

570
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:20,559
Mathematically, we derive these results

571
00:23:18,799 --> 00:23:22,960
from the voltage current relationships

572
00:23:20,559 --> 00:23:25,360
we saw earlier. But phaser notation

573
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:27,520
makes them look just like Ohm's law,

574
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:30,840
only with complex impedance replacing

575
00:23:27,520 --> 00:23:30,840
simple resistance.

576
00:23:32,640 --> 00:23:36,080
Let's learn how to replace two

577
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:38,159
impedances with a single equivalent

578
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:40,159
impedance.

579
00:23:38,159 --> 00:23:42,720
series connection one after the other on

580
00:23:40,159 --> 00:23:46,000
the same branch. The same current flows

581
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:49,600
through both and the voltage drops add.

582
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:52,400
So the equivalent is Z sub E= Z1 plus

583
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:54,400
Z2. This works even when the impedances

584
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:56,799
are complex.

585
00:23:54,400 --> 00:24:00,000
A oneline proof spoken assume a current

586
00:23:56,799 --> 00:24:04,159
I flows. The total voltage is I * Z sub

587
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:09,120
E. It is also I * Z1 plus I * Z2. Set

588
00:24:04,159 --> 00:24:10,960
them equal and cancel Y. Z subb= Z1 + Z2

589
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:13,440
parallel connection. The two elements

590
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:15,520
share the same two nodes. The voltage

591
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:17,200
across each is the same and the currents

592
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:21,200
add.

593
00:24:17,200 --> 00:24:27,039
So the equivalent is 1 / Z sub= 1 / Z1 +

594
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:29,600
1 / Z2 or equivalently Z sub= Z1 * Z2 /

595
00:24:27,039 --> 00:24:31,600
Z1 + Z2.

596
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:34,559
Quick proof spoken. Let the common

597
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:37,919
voltage be V. Currents are I1= sin V

598
00:24:34,559 --> 00:24:42,159
over Z1 and I2= sin V over Z2. Total

599
00:24:37,919 --> 00:24:46,240
current I= sign I1 + I2= sin V * 1 / Z 1

600
00:24:42,159 --> 00:24:48,080
+ 1 / Z2. But I equ= sin V over Z sub E.

601
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:51,080
Cancel V to get the reciprocal form

602
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:51,080
above.

603
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:57,279
Once we know how to combine impedances

604
00:24:54,960 --> 00:25:00,400
into a single equivalent value, we can

605
00:24:57,279 --> 00:25:02,559
simplify any branch of a network.

606
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,960
Each branch can then be treated as just

607
00:25:02,559 --> 00:25:06,799
two things, an impedance and a source.

608
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:09,039
Either a voltage source, a current

609
00:25:06,799 --> 00:25:10,799
source, or both. If there are more

610
00:25:09,039 --> 00:25:12,559
components in the branch, we simply

611
00:25:10,799 --> 00:25:15,520
combine them into one equivalent

612
00:25:12,559 --> 00:25:17,360
impedance before solving.

613
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:20,400
After that, solving the network becomes

614
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:22,320
a two-step process. Step one, find the

615
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:25,440
overall currents and voltages using the

616
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:27,200
simplified equivalent impedances.

617
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:29,039
Step two, if needed, break the

618
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:30,880
equivalent impedance back into its

619
00:25:29,039 --> 00:25:33,200
original components and distribute the

620
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:35,120
results to each part.

621
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:37,360
Now, let's apply this to the idea of a

622
00:25:35,120 --> 00:25:39,279
voltage divider. In the circuit on the

623
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:42,240
left, we have an input voltage split

624
00:25:39,279 --> 00:25:44,080
between a resistor and a capacitor.

625
00:25:42,240 --> 00:25:46,320
The fraction of the input voltage that

626
00:25:44,080 --> 00:25:49,200
appears across the capacitor is given by

627
00:25:46,320 --> 00:25:53,760
the transfer function. Capital T of

628
00:25:49,200 --> 00:25:58,000
omega= 1 / J omega C all over R + 1 / J

629
00:25:53,760 --> 00:26:02,159
omega C. If we simplify that becomes T

630
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:07,840
of omega = 1 / 1 + J * omega over omega

631
00:26:02,159 --> 00:26:09,679
sub C where omega sub C = 1 / R * C.

632
00:26:07,840 --> 00:26:12,159
This formula tells us how the circuit

633
00:26:09,679 --> 00:26:14,799
responds to different frequencies. If

634
00:26:12,159 --> 00:26:16,880
the input is a pure sinosoidal wave, we

635
00:26:14,799 --> 00:26:20,080
just plug its frequency into omega and

636
00:26:16,880 --> 00:26:21,840
get the scaling factor for the output.

637
00:26:20,080 --> 00:26:24,320
And if the input is an arbitrary

638
00:26:21,840 --> 00:26:26,720
waveform, forier analysis lets us break

639
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:29,120
it into many sinosidal components, each

640
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:31,279
with its own frequency.

641
00:26:29,120 --> 00:26:33,679
We apply the transfer function to each

642
00:26:31,279 --> 00:26:36,559
frequency separately, then sum them back

643
00:26:33,679 --> 00:26:38,240
up to get the total output.

644
00:26:36,559 --> 00:26:40,720
This particular circuit is called a

645
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:42,320
lowass filter because it passes low

646
00:26:40,720 --> 00:26:45,039
frequency signals with little

647
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:47,520
attenuation but strongly reduces high

648
00:26:45,039 --> 00:26:49,919
frequency components.

649
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:51,760
At very low frequencies, omega is close

650
00:26:49,919 --> 00:26:53,919
to zero. So the transfer function is

651
00:26:51,760 --> 00:26:56,559
close to one. The output nearly matches

652
00:26:53,919 --> 00:26:58,559
the input. At very high frequencies,

653
00:26:56,559 --> 00:27:00,799
omega is large. The transfer function

654
00:26:58,559 --> 00:27:02,799
becomes very small and the output is

655
00:27:00,799 --> 00:27:04,320
greatly reduced.

656
00:27:02,799 --> 00:27:05,919
The inductor resistor version on the

657
00:27:04,320 --> 00:27:12,039
right works the same way except it's cut

658
00:27:05,919 --> 00:27:12,039
off frequency omega subc= r / l.

659
00:27:13,279 --> 00:27:17,440
When we move into the forier domain any

660
00:27:15,760 --> 00:27:19,760
signal can be represented as a

661
00:27:17,440 --> 00:27:21,760
collection of frequency components.

662
00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:23,679
Filters are circuits or systems that

663
00:27:21,760 --> 00:27:26,799
selectively pass or block these

664
00:27:23,679 --> 00:27:29,279
components based on their frequency.

665
00:27:26,799 --> 00:27:30,799
A lowass filter allows low-frequency

666
00:27:29,279 --> 00:27:33,039
components to pass through while

667
00:27:30,799 --> 00:27:34,960
attenuating or reducing highfrequency

668
00:27:33,039 --> 00:27:37,600
components.

669
00:27:34,960 --> 00:27:40,159
In the idealized diagram here, the pass

670
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:43,200
band is shown as a flat rectangle up to

671
00:27:40,159 --> 00:27:46,159
the cutff frequency omega subc and then

672
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:48,000
it drops to zero in the stop band. In

673
00:27:46,159 --> 00:27:50,159
real circuits, the transition is not

674
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,640
perfectly sharp. It's more gradual, but

675
00:27:50,159 --> 00:27:54,399
the concept is the same. At the cutff

676
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:58,640
frequency, the signal power has dropped

677
00:27:54,399 --> 00:28:00,880
to half its low frequency value.

678
00:27:58,640 --> 00:28:02,960
A highp pass filter does the opposite.

679
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:05,919
It blocks low frequency signals and

680
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:08,000
passes the high frequency ones.

681
00:28:05,919 --> 00:28:10,000
A band pass filter only allows

682
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:12,080
frequencies within a certain range to

683
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:14,080
pass, blocking both lower and higher

684
00:28:12,080 --> 00:28:16,320
frequencies.

685
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:18,640
A band stop filter, sometimes called a

686
00:28:16,320 --> 00:28:20,559
notch filter, removes frequencies within

687
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:23,039
a specific range, but passes those

688
00:28:20,559 --> 00:28:25,200
outside that range.

689
00:28:23,039 --> 00:28:27,679
All of these can be analyzed using the

690
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:30,000
same approach. Decompose the input

691
00:28:27,679 --> 00:28:32,480
signal into sinosoidal components using

692
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:34,399
Forier analysis.

693
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:36,480
For each frequency, calculate the

694
00:28:34,399 --> 00:28:39,200
systems response using impedance in the

695
00:28:36,480 --> 00:28:41,919
phaser form of Ohm's law. Voltage equals

696
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:43,760
impedance time current. combine the

697
00:28:41,919 --> 00:28:46,480
individual frequency responses to

698
00:28:43,760 --> 00:28:48,799
reconstruct the total output.

699
00:28:46,480 --> 00:28:51,120
For DC circuits with constant voltages,

700
00:28:48,799 --> 00:28:53,679
we only need algebraic equations, just

701
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:55,600
resistance time current equals voltage.

702
00:28:53,679 --> 00:28:57,520
But with AC signals or sinosoidal

703
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:59,760
sources, the relationships involve

704
00:28:57,520 --> 00:29:01,919
derivatives and integrals. Phaser

705
00:28:59,760 --> 00:29:04,399
notation transforms those into simple

706
00:29:01,919 --> 00:29:06,480
algebraic equations, letting us solve AC

707
00:29:04,399 --> 00:29:09,480
problems with the same ease as DC

708
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:09,480
problems.

709
00:29:11,120 --> 00:29:14,640
In this sense, an alternating current

710
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:16,720
circuit can be treated just like a

711
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:19,279
direct current circuit. Once we use

712
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:22,640
phaser notation and impedance, that's

713
00:29:19,279 --> 00:29:24,960
why we say AC is DC in disguise.

714
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:26,880
By representing AC voltages and currents

715
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:28,960
as phasers, we turn differential

716
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:31,279
equations into simple algebraic ones

717
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:33,200
just like in DC analysis. This

718
00:29:31,279 --> 00:29:36,320
dramatically simplifies the process of

719
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:38,240
solving AC circuit problems.

720
00:29:36,320 --> 00:29:40,720
This clever idea was first developed

721
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:42,640
decades ago by an engineer at the GE

722
00:29:40,720 --> 00:29:44,240
Global Research Center not far from

723
00:29:42,640 --> 00:29:46,240
here.

724
00:29:44,240 --> 00:29:48,080
It's now a standard computational tool

725
00:29:46,240 --> 00:29:50,159
in electrical engineering and it's one

726
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:53,480
of the reasons AC circuit analysis can

727
00:29:50,159 --> 00:29:53,480
be so elegant.

728
00:29:55,039 --> 00:29:59,840
Here's a very typical and practical

729
00:29:56,799 --> 00:30:01,840
circuit, the voltage divider.

730
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:03,360
We start with an input voltage which is

731
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:06,880
split between two resistors or

732
00:30:03,360 --> 00:30:08,880
impedances R1 and R2. The voltage drop

733
00:30:06,880 --> 00:30:11,360
across the second resistor becomes our

734
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:13,360
output signal. This is a common way to

735
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:15,279
pass a signal from one stage of a system

736
00:30:13,360 --> 00:30:17,360
to the next.

737
00:30:15,279 --> 00:30:20,320
A real world example might be a sensor

738
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:22,159
connected to a measurement device.

739
00:30:20,320 --> 00:30:24,480
The human body, for instance, has its

740
00:30:22,159 --> 00:30:26,399
own electrical impedance. If we were

741
00:30:24,480 --> 00:30:29,200
measuring a cardiac signal, we could

742
00:30:26,399 --> 00:30:31,600
model the body's impedance as R1. The

743
00:30:29,200 --> 00:30:33,360
second resistor R2 could represent the

744
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:35,120
input impedance of our measuring

745
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:36,799
instrument.

746
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:38,640
From an engineering perspective, we

747
00:30:36,799 --> 00:30:41,440
usually have three goals when designing

748
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:43,520
such a circuit. Large input resistance.

749
00:30:41,440 --> 00:30:45,679
We want R1 to be large so that most of

750
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:47,440
the input voltage appears across it,

751
00:30:45,679 --> 00:30:49,760
ensuring we capture as much of the

752
00:30:47,440 --> 00:30:52,559
signal as possible.

753
00:30:49,760 --> 00:30:54,159
Small output resistance. We want R2 to

754
00:30:52,559 --> 00:30:55,679
be small so the next stage in the

755
00:30:54,159 --> 00:30:58,159
circuit can take the signal without

756
00:30:55,679 --> 00:31:00,240
losing much voltage.

757
00:30:58,159 --> 00:31:02,240
high gain. If possible, we want to

758
00:31:00,240 --> 00:31:04,799
amplify the signal to make it easier to

759
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:06,799
detect and process.

760
00:31:04,799 --> 00:31:08,880
These goals can sometimes conflict. For

761
00:31:06,799 --> 00:31:10,880
example, making R1 large helps with

762
00:31:08,880 --> 00:31:13,679
capturing the signal, but we also want

763
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:15,760
R2 small for driving the next stage.

764
00:31:13,679 --> 00:31:17,600
Circuit design is about balancing these

765
00:31:15,760 --> 00:31:20,080
trade-offs.

766
00:31:17,600 --> 00:31:22,399
In the example at the bottom right, RS

767
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:24,720
is our sensor whose resistance changes

768
00:31:22,399 --> 00:31:26,960
slightly by a factor of X in response to

769
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:29,600
something like temperature or pressure.

770
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:31,360
RL is the load resistor. If X equals

771
00:31:29,600 --> 00:31:33,840
zero, the sensor is at its nominal

772
00:31:31,360 --> 00:31:35,840
value. When X changes even by a small

773
00:31:33,840 --> 00:31:37,519
amount, the output voltage V out

774
00:31:35,840 --> 00:31:39,919
changes.

775
00:31:37,519 --> 00:31:43,519
Mathematically, V out equals V in

776
00:31:39,919 --> 00:31:45,440
multiplied by the ratio R2 / R1 + R2.

777
00:31:43,519 --> 00:31:47,760
This is just the voltage divider rule

778
00:31:45,440 --> 00:31:50,240
we've seen before. In practice, the

779
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:52,799
change in X might be very small. So, the

780
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:54,960
change in V out is tiny, often just a

781
00:31:52,799 --> 00:31:57,360
few mills.

782
00:31:54,960 --> 00:31:59,120
If we want to amplify this tiny change,

783
00:31:57,360 --> 00:32:00,880
we ideally remove the constant

784
00:31:59,120 --> 00:32:03,360
background voltage so that only the

785
00:32:00,880 --> 00:32:05,200
small variation gets amplified. This

786
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:09,000
allows for much higher gain without

787
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:09,000
overloading the system.

788
00:32:10,559 --> 00:32:14,720
One way to remove the background signal

789
00:32:12,559 --> 00:32:17,840
and make small changes easier to detect

790
00:32:14,720 --> 00:32:19,760
is by using a Wheatstone bridge.

791
00:32:17,840 --> 00:32:22,480
The Wheatstone bridge is essentially two

792
00:32:19,760 --> 00:32:24,399
voltage dividers connected side by side.

793
00:32:22,480 --> 00:32:27,200
On the left divider, we have resistors

794
00:32:24,399 --> 00:32:30,480
R1 and R2. On the right divider, we have

795
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:32,480
resistors R3 and R4. The output voltage

796
00:32:30,480 --> 00:32:35,519
V underscore out is measured between the

797
00:32:32,480 --> 00:32:37,440
midpoints of these two dividers.

798
00:32:35,519 --> 00:32:42,000
If the ratios of the resistors are

799
00:32:37,440 --> 00:32:43,840
chosen so that R1 / R2 equals R4 / R3,

800
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:46,159
the two dividers produce exactly the

801
00:32:43,840 --> 00:32:48,720
same voltage at their midpoints and V

802
00:32:46,159 --> 00:32:51,039
out is zero. This is called the null

803
00:32:48,720 --> 00:32:53,360
mode. no signal at the output when the

804
00:32:51,039 --> 00:32:56,399
bridge is balanced.

805
00:32:53,360 --> 00:32:58,399
Now if one of the resistors typically R3

806
00:32:56,399 --> 00:33:02,559
is actually a sensor whose resistance

807
00:32:58,399 --> 00:33:04,880
changes slightly say from R to R* 1 + X

808
00:33:02,559 --> 00:33:07,760
the bridge becomes unbalanced. This

809
00:33:04,880 --> 00:33:09,760
small change in R3 produces a nonzero V

810
00:33:07,760 --> 00:33:12,320
out even though the original background

811
00:33:09,760 --> 00:33:14,799
voltage was canceled out.

812
00:33:12,320 --> 00:33:17,919
In deflection mode, we can write V out

813
00:33:14,799 --> 00:33:19,760
as the supply voltage VCC multiplied by

814
00:33:17,919 --> 00:33:22,640
the difference between the left divider

815
00:33:19,760 --> 00:33:24,559
ratio and the right divider ratio.

816
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:26,799
Because the bridge starts balanced, even

817
00:33:24,559 --> 00:33:29,120
a very small change in R3 creates a

818
00:33:26,799 --> 00:33:30,880
measurable output.

819
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:33,120
The advantage is that we've removed the

820
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:35,600
large constant voltage and isolated only

821
00:33:33,120 --> 00:33:37,440
the change we care about. This makes it

822
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:39,200
possible to apply high gain to that

823
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:42,200
small signal without saturating the

824
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:42,200
system.

825
00:33:43,679 --> 00:33:48,159
Up to now, we've looked at components

826
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:50,640
that behave in a linear way. Resistors,

827
00:33:48,159 --> 00:33:52,960
capacitors, and inductors, all of which

828
00:33:50,640 --> 00:33:56,640
follow the generalized Ohm's law.

829
00:33:52,960 --> 00:33:58,720
Voltage equals impedance times current.

830
00:33:56,640 --> 00:34:01,279
But many real world circuit elements are

831
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:02,960
nonlinear components. Their voltage

832
00:34:01,279 --> 00:34:04,880
current relationship does not form a

833
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:06,720
straight line.

834
00:34:04,880 --> 00:34:08,240
For example, a diode only allows

835
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:09,919
significant current to flow in one

836
00:34:08,240 --> 00:34:12,879
direction once the forward voltage

837
00:34:09,919 --> 00:34:15,280
reaches a certain threshold about 0.7

838
00:34:12,879 --> 00:34:17,760
volts for silicon and 0.3 volts for

839
00:34:15,280 --> 00:34:19,440
germanmanium. In reverse bias, very

840
00:34:17,760 --> 00:34:21,280
little current flows until breakdown

841
00:34:19,440 --> 00:34:23,760
occurs.

842
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:25,679
Transistors such as the NPN bipolar

843
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:28,639
junction transistor shown here can

844
00:34:25,679 --> 00:34:30,879
amplify signals by carefully biasing the

845
00:34:28,639 --> 00:34:33,040
base emitter junction. A small change in

846
00:34:30,879 --> 00:34:35,359
base current controls a much larger

847
00:34:33,040 --> 00:34:37,760
change in collector current. This allows

848
00:34:35,359 --> 00:34:40,560
weak input signals to be amplified into

849
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:43,520
stronger output signals.

850
00:34:40,560 --> 00:34:45,919
We also have digital logic gates or and

851
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:49,359
zor norand and not which are the

852
00:34:45,919 --> 00:34:51,760
building blocks of digital electronics.

853
00:34:49,359 --> 00:34:54,480
They process binary signals producing

854
00:34:51,760 --> 00:34:56,560
specific outputs based on logical rules.

855
00:34:54,480 --> 00:34:58,720
By combining these gates, we can create

856
00:34:56,560 --> 00:35:00,240
flip-flops and sequential circuits which

857
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:02,240
store and manipulate digital

858
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:04,480
information.

859
00:35:02,240 --> 00:35:06,800
Nonlinear components make it possible to

860
00:35:04,480 --> 00:35:09,200
build amplifiers, oscillators, logic

861
00:35:06,800 --> 00:35:11,599
circuits, and microprocessors. They open

862
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:13,839
the door to modern electronics where

863
00:35:11,599 --> 00:35:16,079
linear analysis still plays a role but

864
00:35:13,839 --> 00:35:19,880
must be combined with device specific

865
00:35:16,079 --> 00:35:19,880
nonlinear behavior.

866
00:35:21,440 --> 00:35:25,040
Just as we can have linear systems and

867
00:35:23,440 --> 00:35:28,640
nonlinear systems, we also have

868
00:35:25,040 --> 00:35:31,760
nonlinear electronic components. V. One

869
00:35:28,640 --> 00:35:33,599
of the most fundamental is the diode.

870
00:35:31,760 --> 00:35:35,680
A diode has a strong directional

871
00:35:33,599 --> 00:35:37,280
preference for current flow. In its

872
00:35:35,680 --> 00:35:39,040
forward direction, the current rises

873
00:35:37,280 --> 00:35:42,800
rapidly after a certain threshold

874
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:44,320
voltage about 0.7 volts for silicon. In

875
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:46,800
the reverse direction, the current is

876
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:48,960
extremely small until breakdown occurs.

877
00:35:46,800 --> 00:35:51,200
This is a clearly nonlinear voltage

878
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:53,359
current relationship.

879
00:35:51,200 --> 00:35:56,000
If we combine two diodes in a specific

880
00:35:53,359 --> 00:35:58,240
configuration, we create a transistor. A

881
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:00,640
transistor takes a small signal at its

882
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:03,280
input and produces a much larger version

883
00:36:00,640 --> 00:36:06,079
of the same signal shape at its output.

884
00:36:03,280 --> 00:36:07,920
It acts as an amplifier. This ability to

885
00:36:06,079 --> 00:36:09,599
magnify weak signals makes the

886
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:11,839
transistor an essential building block

887
00:36:09,599 --> 00:36:13,839
for electronics.

888
00:36:11,839 --> 00:36:16,800
Multiple transistors can be combined to

889
00:36:13,839 --> 00:36:18,960
perform logic operations.

890
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:21,599
For example, an AND gate outputs a high

891
00:36:18,960 --> 00:36:23,839
voltage only if both inputs are high. An

892
00:36:21,599 --> 00:36:26,400
O gate outputs a high voltage if either

893
00:36:23,839 --> 00:36:28,800
input is high. More complex gates like

894
00:36:26,400 --> 00:36:31,119
NANDOR and Exor can be built from these

895
00:36:28,800 --> 00:36:33,599
basic ideas.

896
00:36:31,119 --> 00:36:36,160
By connecting logic gates, we can create

897
00:36:33,599 --> 00:36:38,560
circuits that add, multiply, store data

898
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:41,040
and make decisions. The very basis of

899
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:42,960
digital computers.

900
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:44,960
This is why in electronics we need to

901
00:36:42,960 --> 00:36:46,720
understand both linear and nonlinear

902
00:36:44,960 --> 00:36:48,640
systems.

903
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:51,040
Linear components handle predictable

904
00:36:48,640 --> 00:36:52,800
analog relationships while nonlinear

905
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:54,720
components give us amplification,

906
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:58,200
switching and the foundation for all

907
00:36:54,720 --> 00:36:58,200
digital processing.

908
00:36:59,839 --> 00:37:04,000
As we just discussed, we often want

909
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:06,640
three things in a circuit.

910
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:09,359
very high input resistance, very low

911
00:37:06,640 --> 00:37:11,680
output resistance, and a large gain or

912
00:37:09,359 --> 00:37:14,079
magnification factor.

913
00:37:11,680 --> 00:37:17,040
The operational amplifier or op amp

914
00:37:14,079 --> 00:37:18,720
delivers exactly that. Inside an op amp

915
00:37:17,040 --> 00:37:20,640
are many transistors and other

916
00:37:18,720 --> 00:37:22,560
components all arranged to meet these

917
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:24,240
requirements.

918
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:26,320
The internal circuitry can look

919
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:28,880
complicated, but for analysis, we can

920
00:37:26,320 --> 00:37:30,560
use a simple model. We apply a small

921
00:37:28,880 --> 00:37:32,880
input voltage V in between the

922
00:37:30,560 --> 00:37:35,200
non-inverting and inverting inputs. The

923
00:37:32,880 --> 00:37:37,839
op amp multiplies this by a large gain

924
00:37:35,200 --> 00:37:40,960
factor G to produce the output voltage V

925
00:37:37,839 --> 00:37:43,359
out equals G * VIN.

926
00:37:40,960 --> 00:37:45,520
In the ideal model, the gain is

927
00:37:43,359 --> 00:37:47,599
infinitely large.

928
00:37:45,520 --> 00:37:49,359
The input impedance is infinite. So the

929
00:37:47,599 --> 00:37:52,560
op amp doesn't draw current from the

930
00:37:49,359 --> 00:37:54,640
source. We capture the full signal.

931
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:56,320
The output impedance is zero. So the op

932
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:58,960
amp can drive the next stage without

933
00:37:56,320 --> 00:38:01,280
loss. Real op amps don't reach these

934
00:37:58,960 --> 00:38:03,520
ideal values, but they can come close.

935
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:06,240
For example, input impedance in the mega

936
00:38:03,520 --> 00:38:08,640
to ohm range and output impedance as low

937
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:11,040
as a few hundred ohms.

938
00:38:08,640 --> 00:38:12,960
An op amp is an active component. It

939
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:16,320
needs its own power supply, typically

940
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:18,960
labeled VS plus and VS minus. This

941
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:20,720
internal power lets it amplify signals,

942
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:22,880
something passive components like

943
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:26,839
resistors, capacitors, and inductors

944
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:26,839
cannot do on their own.

945
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:32,480
Even though an operational amplifier is

946
00:38:30,400 --> 00:38:35,200
an active component, the basic circuit

947
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:39,040
laws still apply, Kirchoff's current law

948
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:41,119
and Kirchoff's voltage law always hold.

949
00:38:39,040 --> 00:38:43,760
But for op amps, we have two special

950
00:38:41,119 --> 00:38:45,839
rules that make analysis much easier.

951
00:38:43,760 --> 00:38:47,920
Rule one, the two input terminals are at

952
00:38:45,839 --> 00:38:49,599
the same voltage.

953
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:52,160
This happens because the op amp's gain

954
00:38:49,599 --> 00:38:53,520
is extremely large. Even the tiniest

955
00:38:52,160 --> 00:38:55,440
difference between the inputs is

956
00:38:53,520 --> 00:38:57,119
amplified so much that the output

957
00:38:55,440 --> 00:38:59,839
adjusts to make the two inputs

958
00:38:57,119 --> 00:39:02,000
essentially equal in voltage.

959
00:38:59,839 --> 00:39:04,480
Rule two, no current flows into either

960
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:07,200
input terminal. The input impedance of

961
00:39:04,480 --> 00:39:09,280
an op amp is extremely high, ideally

962
00:39:07,200 --> 00:39:11,680
infinite, so no measurable current

963
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:13,520
enters the inputs.

964
00:39:11,680 --> 00:39:16,400
These two rules are the foundation for

965
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:18,640
almost all op amp circuit analysis. They

966
00:39:16,400 --> 00:39:20,880
let us treat the inputs as if they are

967
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:23,359
at the same voltage and as if they draw

968
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:28,040
no current, which simplifies solving

969
00:39:23,359 --> 00:39:28,040
even complex amplifier circuits.

970
00:39:29,760 --> 00:39:34,720
Let's look at this inverting amplifier

971
00:39:32,480 --> 00:39:38,720
and walk through the analysis step by

972
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:40,640
step using our two op amp rules.

973
00:39:38,720 --> 00:39:43,200
First, the non-inverting input is

974
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:45,280
connected to ground. By rule one, the

975
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:47,760
inverting input is also at 0 volts.

976
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:49,760
That's our virtual ground.

977
00:39:47,760 --> 00:39:52,000
By rule two, no current flows into the

978
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:53,359
op amp input.

979
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:55,359
That means all the current coming

980
00:39:53,359 --> 00:39:57,119
through the input resistor RAR from the

981
00:39:55,359 --> 00:40:00,480
input voltage must flow through the

982
00:39:57,119 --> 00:40:02,480
feedback resistor RF to the output.

983
00:40:00,480 --> 00:40:06,320
Now the current through RA equals the

984
00:40:02,480 --> 00:40:08,720
input voltage minus 0 / RA. The current

985
00:40:06,320 --> 00:40:11,520
through RF equals 0 minus the output

986
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:13,359
voltage divided RF.

987
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:16,640
Since these currents are the same, we

988
00:40:13,359 --> 00:40:20,160
can say input voltage / RA equals

989
00:40:16,640 --> 00:40:23,280
negative output voltage / RF.

990
00:40:20,160 --> 00:40:26,320
If we multiply both sides by RF, we get

991
00:40:23,280 --> 00:40:28,560
output voltage equals negative RF / R*

992
00:40:26,320 --> 00:40:30,320
the input voltage.

993
00:40:28,560 --> 00:40:32,720
The negative sign means the output is

994
00:40:30,320 --> 00:40:34,960
inverted compared to the input. The size

995
00:40:32,720 --> 00:40:37,599
of the gain is the value of RF divided

996
00:40:34,960 --> 00:40:40,480
by the value of RA.

997
00:40:37,599 --> 00:40:42,320
So with just those two op amp rules, we

998
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:44,079
can find the input output relationship

999
00:40:42,320 --> 00:40:47,560
without worrying about the op amp's

1000
00:40:44,079 --> 00:40:47,560
internal complexity.

1001
00:40:49,359 --> 00:40:54,079
Operational amplifiers can be connected

1002
00:40:51,599 --> 00:40:57,119
in many different configurations, each

1003
00:40:54,079 --> 00:40:59,680
producing a specific function.

1004
00:40:57,119 --> 00:41:01,599
Here you see several common modules. A

1005
00:40:59,680 --> 00:41:06,240
non-inverting amplifier where the gain

1006
00:41:01,599 --> 00:41:08,319
equals 1 + the ratio of R2 over R1.

1007
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:11,440
An inverting amplifier where the gain

1008
00:41:08,319 --> 00:41:13,440
equals negative R2 / R1. A

1009
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:15,119
differentiator which produces an output

1010
00:41:13,440 --> 00:41:17,440
proportional to the rate of change of

1011
00:41:15,119 --> 00:41:19,440
the input signal.

1012
00:41:17,440 --> 00:41:21,200
An integrator which produces an output

1013
00:41:19,440 --> 00:41:23,839
proportional to the time integral of the

1014
00:41:21,200 --> 00:41:25,520
input signal. A differential amplifier

1015
00:41:23,839 --> 00:41:28,079
which amplifies the difference between

1016
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:30,079
two input signals.

1017
00:41:28,079 --> 00:41:32,000
All of these can be analyzed using the

1018
00:41:30,079 --> 00:41:34,000
same two op amp rules we discussed

1019
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:36,400
earlier. The inputs are at the same

1020
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:38,800
voltage and no current flows into the

1021
00:41:36,400 --> 00:41:40,560
input terminals.

1022
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:42,800
While the details of each configuration

1023
00:41:40,560 --> 00:41:44,079
can get more involved, for our purposes,

1024
00:41:42,800 --> 00:41:46,319
it's enough to understand the

1025
00:41:44,079 --> 00:41:48,400
fundamental idea. By choosing how we

1026
00:41:46,319 --> 00:41:50,640
connect resistors, capacitors, and

1027
00:41:48,400 --> 00:41:52,800
feedback paths, we can make the op amp

1028
00:41:50,640 --> 00:41:55,200
perform a wide range of analog signal

1029
00:41:52,800 --> 00:41:56,640
processing tasks.

1030
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:58,880
If you're interested in the full

1031
00:41:56,640 --> 00:42:01,359
derivations and formulas, the linked

1032
00:41:58,880 --> 00:42:03,520
reference has complete explanations. But

1033
00:42:01,359 --> 00:42:07,240
for this class, understanding the basic

1034
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:07,240
concepts is sufficient.

1035
00:42:08,880 --> 00:42:12,640
We can also use operational amplifiers

1036
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:15,040
in measurement setups, particularly for

1037
00:42:12,640 --> 00:42:16,560
impedance matching.

1038
00:42:15,040 --> 00:42:18,720
Looking into the input port of an op

1039
00:42:16,560 --> 00:42:20,400
amp, the impedance is very high. This

1040
00:42:18,720 --> 00:42:23,359
means it can capture a signal from a

1041
00:42:20,400 --> 00:42:24,960
source without significantly loading it.

1042
00:42:23,359 --> 00:42:26,480
On the other hand, when we take the

1043
00:42:24,960 --> 00:42:28,960
signal from the output port, the

1044
00:42:26,480 --> 00:42:30,400
impedance is very low. This allows the

1045
00:42:28,960 --> 00:42:32,800
signal to drive the next stage

1046
00:42:30,400 --> 00:42:34,800
efficiently. In this way, the op amp

1047
00:42:32,800 --> 00:42:36,720
satisfies the two opposing requirements

1048
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:39,920
we discussed earlier, high input

1049
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:42,079
impedance and low output impedance.

1050
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:44,720
This principle applies to both DC and AC

1051
00:42:42,079 --> 00:42:46,560
circuit analysis. Remember the voltage

1052
00:42:44,720 --> 00:42:48,800
current relationships for resistors,

1053
00:42:46,560 --> 00:42:50,880
capacitors, and inductors. When you

1054
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:53,359
connect these components into a network,

1055
00:42:50,880 --> 00:42:55,760
Kirchhoff's voltage law and Kirchoff's

1056
00:42:53,359 --> 00:42:58,640
current law give you enough equations to

1057
00:42:55,760 --> 00:43:00,400
solve for all unknowns.

1058
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:02,720
The beauty of phaser notation is that

1059
00:43:00,400 --> 00:43:05,280
for AC steadystate circuits, you don't

1060
00:43:02,720 --> 00:43:07,440
need to solve differential equations.

1061
00:43:05,280 --> 00:43:10,480
You simply work with algebraic equations

1062
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:13,280
just as in DC analysis. This is why in a

1063
00:43:10,480 --> 00:43:16,319
sense we can say AC is DC in disguise at

1064
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:17,760
least for steady state analysis.

1065
00:43:16,319 --> 00:43:19,839
Of course, if you're dealing with

1066
00:43:17,760 --> 00:43:21,839
transients, for example, when a signal

1067
00:43:19,839 --> 00:43:23,599
is suddenly applied, you would still

1068
00:43:21,839 --> 00:43:26,079
need to solve the time domain

1069
00:43:23,599 --> 00:43:28,240
differential equations. But once the

1070
00:43:26,079 --> 00:43:30,480
system reaches steady state, algebraic

1071
00:43:28,240 --> 00:43:32,480
methods are enough.

1072
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:34,800
These circuit concepts are applied in

1073
00:43:32,480 --> 00:43:37,040
many areas from sensing signals in

1074
00:43:34,800 --> 00:43:38,880
medical imaging to filtering unwanted

1075
00:43:37,040 --> 00:43:40,560
noise.

1076
00:43:38,880 --> 00:43:42,480
In fact, we've already seen examples

1077
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:44,720
where Forier analysis helps us

1078
00:43:42,480 --> 00:43:47,119
understand how circuits pass or block

1079
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:49,280
certain frequencies. This is crucial for

1080
00:43:47,119 --> 00:43:51,280
designing data acquisition systems that

1081
00:43:49,280 --> 00:43:54,280
can detect and process signals

1082
00:43:51,280 --> 00:43:54,280
effectively.

