1482
00:58:54,880 --> 00:58:57,760
And when we're working with

1483
00:58:55,920 --> 00:59:00,079
oscillations, waveforms, or

1484
00:58:57,760 --> 00:59:02,640
electromagnetic signals, concepts from

1485
00:59:00,079 --> 00:59:05,040
complex analysis such as amplitude and

1486
00:59:02,640 --> 00:59:08,319
phase can be built directly into the

1487
00:59:05,040 --> 00:59:10,400
network using complex valued weights.

1488
00:59:08,319 --> 00:59:13,040
The takeaway here is that neural network

1489
00:59:10,400 --> 00:59:14,720
design is not guesswork. It's about

1490
00:59:13,040 --> 00:59:16,799
choosing the right principles from

1491
00:59:14,720 --> 00:59:18,720
mathematics and engineering and

1492
00:59:16,799 --> 00:59:20,799
embedding them into the architecture so

1493
00:59:18,720 --> 00:59:23,799
that it's well matched to the problem at

1494
00:59:20,799 --> 00:59:23,799
hand.

1495
00:59:25,200 --> 00:59:30,319
Now, how do we actually optimize a

1496
00:59:26,960 --> 00:59:31,839
neural network so that it performs well?

1497
00:59:30,319 --> 00:59:33,920
As I mentioned earlier, when you start

1498
00:59:31,839 --> 00:59:35,920
with a fresh network, its performance is

1499
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:38,079
usually terrible. The outputs are

1500
00:59:35,920 --> 00:59:40,240
essentially random.

1501
00:59:38,079 --> 00:59:42,400
The idea is to adjust the parameters,

1502
00:59:40,240 --> 00:59:44,640
the weights, which we represent as a

1503
00:59:42,400 --> 00:59:46,559
vector W, in such a way that the

1504
00:59:44,640 --> 00:59:49,680
network's predictions get closer and

1505
00:59:46,559 --> 00:59:51,280
closer to the desired results.

1506
00:59:49,680 --> 00:59:53,280
We measure the difference between the

1507
00:59:51,280 --> 00:59:55,119
network's current output and the target

1508
00:59:53,280 --> 00:59:57,599
output using what we call a loss

1509
00:59:55,119 --> 00:59:59,440
function or an error function. The goal

1510
00:59:57,599 --> 01:00:01,200
is to make this error as small as

1511
00:59:59,440 --> 01:00:04,480
possible.

1512
01:00:01,200 --> 01:00:06,400
One common approach is gradient descent.

1513
01:00:04,480 --> 01:00:08,720
We start with an initial set of weights

1514
01:00:06,400 --> 01:00:10,559
often chosen randomly. Then in each

1515
01:00:08,720 --> 01:00:12,400
training step, we look at the slope of

1516
01:00:10,559 --> 01:00:15,200
the error function with respect to each

1517
01:00:12,400 --> 01:00:17,440
weight. This slope or gradient tells us

1518
01:00:15,200 --> 01:00:19,119
which direction increases the error. So

1519
01:00:17,440 --> 01:00:21,359
we move in the opposite direction

1520
01:00:19,119 --> 01:00:23,200
reducing the error.

1521
01:00:21,359 --> 01:00:25,680
Mathematically the new weight vector

1522
01:00:23,200 --> 01:00:27,839
equals the old weight vector minus a

1523
01:00:25,680 --> 01:00:29,440
small fraction of the gradient. That

1524
01:00:27,839 --> 01:00:31,760
fraction is controlled by the learning

1525
01:00:29,440 --> 01:00:34,000
rate, a simple scaling factor between

1526
01:00:31,760 --> 01:00:35,760
zero and one.

1527
01:00:34,000 --> 01:00:37,680
If the learning rate is too big, the

1528
01:00:35,760 --> 01:00:40,240
updates may overshoot and the training

1529
01:00:37,680 --> 01:00:42,880
becomes unstable. If it's too small, the

1530
01:00:40,240 --> 01:00:44,720
network learns very slowly.

1531
01:00:42,880 --> 01:00:46,880
By repeatedly updating the weights in

1532
01:00:44,720 --> 01:00:49,200
this way, the error decreases step by

1533
01:00:46,880 --> 01:00:51,440
step. Eventually, if everything is set

1534
01:00:49,200 --> 01:00:55,880
up well, the process converges and the

1535
01:00:51,440 --> 01:00:55,880
network produces accurate outputs.

